```mermaid
erDiagram
    USER ||--o{ POST : writes
    POST ||--o{ COMMENT : has
    POST }o--o{ TAG : tagged_with
    USER {
        bigint user_id PK
        string name
        string email
    }
    POST {
        bigint post_id PK
        bigint user_id FK
        string title
    }
    COMMENT {
        bigint comment_id PK
        bigint post_id FK
        string body
    }
    TAG {
        bigint tag_id PK
        string label
    }
```

The crow's-foot notation reads as cardinality: `||` is exactly one, `o{` is zero-or-many. One USER writes many POSTs (1:N), and the POST-to-TAG link is many-to-many (M:N), which a junction table will resolve.

```mermaid
erDiagram
    USER ||--|| PROFILE : has
    USER ||--o{ POST : writes
    POST }o--o{ TAG : tagged_with
    USER {
        bigint user_id PK
    }
    PROFILE {
        bigint profile_id PK
        bigint user_id FK
    }
    POST {
        bigint post_id PK
        bigint user_id FK
    }
    TAG {
        bigint tag_id PK
    }
```

Read the crow's feet for the count: USER-to-PROFILE is one-to-one (`||--||`), USER-to-POST is one-to-many (`||--o{`), and POST-to-TAG is many-to-many (`}o--o{`). The 1:N foreign key sits in POST; the M:N link needs a separate junction table.

-- 1:1 — User has one Profile
CREATE TABLE "user" (
    user_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    username VARCHAR(50) UNIQUE NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE profile (
    profile_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    bio TEXT,
    user_id BIGINT UNIQUE NOT NULL,  -- FK on dependent side, UNIQUE enforces 1:1
    CONSTRAINT fk_user FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES "user"(user_id)
);

-- 1:N — Department has many Employees
CREATE TABLE department (
    dept_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    dept_name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE employee (
    emp_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    emp_name VARCHAR(100),
    dept_id BIGINT NOT NULL,         -- FK on the N side (many employees)
    CONSTRAINT fk_dept FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES department(dept_id)
);

-- M:N — Students enroll in Courses
CREATE TABLE student (student_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100));
CREATE TABLE course (course_id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY, title VARCHAR(100));

CREATE TABLE enrollment (            -- Junction table for M:N
    student_id BIGINT,
    course_id BIGINT,
    enrolled_date DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE,
    PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id),
    CONSTRAINT fk_student FOREIGN KEY (student_id) REFERENCES student(student_id),
    CONSTRAINT fk_course FOREIGN KEY (course_id) REFERENCES course(course_id)
);

```mermaid
erDiagram
    POST ||--o{ POST_TAG : has
    TAG  ||--o{ POST_TAG : has
    POST {
        bigint post_id PK
        string title
    }
    TAG {
        bigint tag_id PK
        string label
    }
    POST_TAG {
        bigint post_id PK "FK"
        bigint tag_id PK "FK"
        timestamp tagged_at
    }
```

The junction table POST_TAG sits in the middle. POST has many POST_TAG rows and TAG has many POST_TAG rows, so two one-to-many links together express the original many-to-many. The composite primary key (post_id, tag_id) keeps each pair unique.

```mermaid
erDiagram
    POST ||--|{ COMMENT : "must belong to (total)"
    USER ||--o{ POST : "may write (partial)"
    POST {
        bigint post_id PK
    }
    COMMENT {
        bigint comment_id PK
        bigint post_id FK "NOT NULL"
    }
    USER {
        bigint user_id PK
    }
```

The `|{` end on COMMENT marks total participation: every comment must attach to a post, so post_id is NOT NULL. The `o{` end on POST marks partial participation: a user may write zero posts, so that side stays optional.

```mermaid
erDiagram
    USER ||--o{ POST : writes
    POST ||--o{ COMMENT : has
    POST }o--o{ TAG : tagged_with
    USER {
        bigint user_id PK
        string name
    }
    POST {
        bigint post_id PK
        bigint user_id FK
    }
    COMMENT {
        bigint comment_id PK
        bigint post_id FK
    }
    TAG {
        bigint tag_id PK
    }
```

Apply the rules straight off the diagram: each box becomes a table, each one-to-many edge drops a foreign key on the many side (user_id in POST, post_id in COMMENT), and the many-to-many POST-TAG edge becomes a new post_tags junction table.
